Subject: Shortest decimal of a power of two: try the neighbour across d when the nearest misses

What this does
  In js_dtoa's free format (Number.prototype.toString and JSON.stringify outside 1e-6..1e15, and every |d| < 1e-100 through the snprintf search of 0053), when d is a normal power of two and the nearest decimal of P digits does not read back as d, its neighbours are tried before deciding that P digits are too few. The snprintf search keeps the digits of its last success instead of formatting the answer again.

Why we need it
  Spec bug (MSG 985): String(2 ** -24) gave 5.9604644775390625e-8, but 5.960464477539063e-8 is shorter and reads back as the same double (node 24 prints it). At a power of two the gap below d is half the gap above, so the nearest P-digit decimal (a tie rounded to even here) can lie just outside the interval below d while the one above is inside. Against node 24 on 412,587 values (every power of two 2^-1074..2^1023 with neighbours and odd multiples, 300,000 random finite doubles over all exponents, 100,000 subnormals) the old output differed in 102, the new in none. The free-format path is also 2.6% fewer cycles on a wide-range String() benchmark (no second formatting of the answer).

Soundness
  With a symmetric interval, if the nearest P-digit decimal is outside it the one across d is at least as far, so it is outside too: only a power of two (lopsided interval) needs the extra test, and only the neighbour across d can then be inside, so it is the nearest valid one. Candidates outside [radix^(P-1), radix^P) are skipped (a carry or a lost digit is a shorter form, found at a smaller P). The search over P stays correct: a valid decimal of P digits gives one of P + 1, so validity is monotone. Differentials identical to the old engine except jdt2's hash, which now equals node's; jpow2.js (412,587 values) equal to node 24.

